import json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]
rows=[]
def add(strand, passage, question, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation):
    rows.append((strand,passage,question,correct,[wrong1,wrong2,wrong3],explanation))
# Practice: rhythm and its effect, with an explicit performance cue where needed.
add('rhythm','Poem: Step, clap! Step, clap! / Boots go down the marching track.','Read “Step, clap!” with one beat on each word. What does this repeated beat help you imagine?','Regular marching steps.','Someone floating silently.','A single sudden crash.','A bird changing direction.', 'The repeated step-and-clap pattern gives the words an even marching beat.')
add('rhythm','Poem: Run, run, race to the gate! / Quick, quick, we must not wait!','How do the short repeated words help convey the action?','They make the movement feel hurried.','They make the runners seem asleep.','They describe the gate’s colour.','They suggest a long, restful pause.','Run and quick are repeated in short bursts, supporting the urgent race.')
add('rhythm','Poem: Rock ... and rest ... / Warm in the nest.','The reader pauses at each set of dots. How does that reading suit the scene?','It slows the movement and sounds restful.','It makes the nest seem noisy and crowded.','It sounds like a fast race.','It tells us how many birds there are.','The pauses stretch the words apart, matching gentle rocking and rest.')
add('rhythm','Poem A: Hop! Stop! Hop! Stop! / Over the stones we pop. / Poem B: Slowly we wander beside the stream.','Which comparison describes the effect of reading A with a sharp beat on each short word?','A feels bouncy and stop-start beside B’s slower wandering.','A and B both describe sleeping.','B has more repeated commands than A.','The sharp beat makes A feel completely still.','The alternating short commands in A imitate hopping and stopping; B describes continuous slow movement.')
add('rhythm','Poem: One small swing, / Then back we bring / Our feet beneath the sky.','Read the first two lines with the same gentle beat. What action can that pattern suggest?','A swing moving out and back.','A glass breaking once.','An engine suddenly stopping.','Rain becoming hail.','A repeated gentle beat can echo the back-and-forth action described by the words.')
add('rhythm','Poem: Tap-tap-tap, the dancers go; / Hold ... then turn, and bow down low.','What does the pause after “Hold” add to the dance description?','It creates a break before the next movement.','It removes every sound from the poem.','It tells us the dancers have left.','It makes all the movements happen at once.','The pause makes the reader wait, just as the dancer holds still before turning.')
add('rhythm','Poem: We paddle, we paddle, we paddle to shore. / We rest our tired arms. No paddling more.','Why does the repeated phrase suit the first line?','It echoes the repeated strokes needed to paddle.','It shows that the boat has no paddles.','It gives the exact distance to shore.','It means the children never become tired.','Repeating the same phrase reflects doing the same paddling action again and again.')
add('rhythm','Poem: Slow footsteps cross the floor ... / A knock! A shout! An open door!','How could a reader show the change from quiet movement to sudden activity?','Begin slowly, then make the short exclamations brisk.','Rush the first line and whisper every later word slowly.','Pause for the same long time after every letter.','Leave out the second line.','The slow footsteps and dots invite a measured opening; the exclamations signal sudden action.')
# Onomatopoeia in both poetry and prose.
add('onomatopoeia','Prose: Clink! The teaspoon struck the glass. Ava looked up.','What does “Clink!” add that “The teaspoon struck the glass” does not supply as directly?','An imitation of the small ringing sound.','The temperature of the drink.','A description of Ava’s clothes.','The reason Ava picked that glass.','Clink imitates the brief ringing noise of metal touching glass.')
add('onomatopoeia','Poem: Drip, drip, into the tin; / One more raindrop tumbles in.','How does repeating “drip” help the reader hear the scene?','It suggests separate drops landing one after another.','It suggests a constant loud explosion.','It says that no rain is falling.','It describes the tin’s bright colour.','Each repeated sound-word can stand for another drop, making the pattern audible in the words.')
add('onomatopoeia','Prose: The branch snapped. Crack! Both walkers stopped.','Why is “Crack!” useful at this moment?','It makes the branch’s sudden breaking sound stand out.','It explains which walker planted the tree.','It makes the walk sound silent.','It tells the reader the branch is soft.','The sharp sound-word highlights a sudden noise that interrupts the walk.')
add('onomatopoeia','Poem: Hiss goes the air from the tyre; / Our busy wheel sinks lower.','Which word lets the reader almost hear the escaping air?','Hiss.','Busy.','Lower.','Wheel.','Hiss imitates the thin, continuing sound of air escaping; the other words describe the object or its state.')
add('onomatopoeia','Prose A: A book fell to the carpet. / Prose B: Thud! A book fell to the carpet.','What changes when the writer adds “Thud!”?','The impact becomes easier to hear in the reader’s imagination.','The book becomes a different colour.','The carpet becomes a wooden floor.','The event happens before the book falls.','Thud adds a dull impact sound while keeping the same event and setting.')
add('onomatopoeia','Poem: Buzz by the blossom, / A bee settles near.','Why does “buzz” suit the creature in this poem?','It imitates the sound associated with the bee’s movement.','It describes the taste of the blossom.','It suggests that the bee is a barking dog.','It gives the bee a human speaking voice.','Buzz is a sound imitation associated with a flying bee, matching the creature named in the next line.')
add('onomatopoeia','Prose: “Shh,” whispered Dad. The baby had finally fallen asleep.','How does the sound-word support Dad’s purpose?','It makes his request for quiet part of the sound of the sentence.','It turns his request into a loud cheer.','It tells us the baby’s name.','It means Dad wants the baby to sing.','Shh imitates a quieting sound, so its effect matches protecting the baby’s sleep.')
add('onomatopoeia','Poem: Splash! My boot found a puddle; / Cold water climbed up my sock.','What can “Splash!” help a reader imagine?','Water scattering when the boot lands.','Dry leaves moving in hot wind.','A clock ringing in another room.','A sock being folded quietly.','Splash imitates the sound of water being disturbed, matching the boot landing in the puddle.')
# Comparisons: meaning AND effect, not merely spotting “like”.
add('simile','Prose: The lake lay like a sheet of glass. Even the reeds were still.','What picture does the comparison create?','A smooth, still water surface.','A lake full of sharp broken pieces.','A lake someone has built from glass.','Water rushing noisily over rocks.','A sheet of glass suggests a flat, smooth surface; the still reeds support this calm picture.')
add('simile','Poem: Her laugh was like a little bell, / Bright across the yard.','What quality does the bell comparison give the laugh?','A clear, ringing sound.','A rough, scratchy feel.','A salty taste.','A dark colour.','The comparison uses a bell’s ringing sound to help the reader imagine the laugh.')
add('simile','Prose: The puppy wriggled like a fish in Mia’s arms.','Which detail does the comparison emphasise?','The puppy’s twisting movement.','The puppy’s ability to breathe underwater.','The shape of Mia’s house.','The colour of the puppy’s food.','A fish’s wriggling movement makes the puppy’s restless motion easier to picture; it is not a literal change into a fish.')
add('simile','Poem: Clouds like cotton drift away; / Soft white shapes above our day.','Why does “cotton” fit the next line?','It reinforces the clouds’ soft, white appearance.','It proves that clouds are made from cloth.','It makes the clouds sound like thunder.','It gives their exact height.','Soft white shapes explains the shared appearance behind the comparison.')
add('simile','Prose: The crowd poured through the gate like water from a tipped bucket.','What does this comparison make the crowd’s movement seem like?','A large flow moving through an opening.','One person waiting without moving.','People carefully hanging buckets.','A group turning into rain.','Pouring water suggests a flow, helping us picture many people passing through together.')
add('simile','Poem: The tiny torch, a star to me, / Shone in the tent’s dark sea.','Does the speaker mean the torch is really a star?','No; the comparison makes its small light seem bright in darkness.','Yes; the torch has become a ball of burning gas.','Yes; the tent is now in outer space.','No; the line means the torch gives no light.','The poem compares the torch with a star to highlight a small light against darkness.')
add('simile','Prose: “The path curved like a ribbon” becomes “The path went around the hill.”','What is lost in the second version?','The comparison that helps us picture a long, bending strip.','The idea that there is a path.','All information about the path’s direction.','The name of a character.','The plain version retains a route around the hill but removes the ribbon image.')
add('simile','Poem: My backpack felt like a boulder / Pulling hard upon my shoulder.','Which reading explains the speaker’s meaning?','The bag feels very heavy to carry.','The bag is definitely made of stone.','The bag can roll itself to school.','The shoulder is made of rock.','A boulder suggests great weight; the next line confirms the effort of carrying the bag.')
# Sensory language and reader effects.
add('sensory detail','Prose: The orange peel released a sharp, fresh scent as Nila opened her lunchbox.','Which experience does this detail bring closest to the reader?','Smelling the peeled orange.','Hearing a lunch bell.','Feeling a smooth desk.','Seeing a dark sky.','Scent names a smell, and sharp and fresh make that smell more specific.')
add('sensory detail','Poem: Grit between my toes, / Warm sand where I stand.','How do these details help create the beach setting?','They let the reader imagine textures and warmth on bare feet.','They tell us the sea is freezing.','They explain why beaches have tides.','They supply the sound of a boat engine.','Grit and warm sand are touch details that place the reader physically in the scene.')
add('sensory detail','Prose: The lemon made his mouth pucker; a sharp sour taste lingered.','Which revision keeps the same sensory effect?','The sour lemon made him screw up his mouth.','The lemon’s smooth yellow skin shone.','The lemon rolled silently away.','The lemon weighed more than the apple.','Sourness and the mouth’s reaction preserve the taste experience; the other choices change the focus.')
add('sensory detail','Poem: A silver trail on a midnight leaf; / The snail slips out of sight.','What does “silver trail” especially help the reader picture?','A pale, shining line against a dark scene.','The snail making a loud crash.','A leaf tasting of metal.','A heavy railway track.','Silver suggests a light, shining appearance, contrasted with midnight darkness.')
add('sensory detail','Prose A: The room was unpleasant. / Prose B: Damp socks smelled sour beside a bin of old food.','Why does B make the unpleasantness more vivid?','It gives particular smells the reader can imagine.','It says that every room is unpleasant.','It removes all objects from the scene.','It tells us exactly who left the socks.','Specific smell details let the reader experience a cause of the unpleasantness rather than only being told an opinion.')
add('sensory detail','Poem: The blanket scratches at my chin; / I tuck the rough edge under.','How does “scratches” affect our understanding of the blanket?','It makes the blanket feel uncomfortable against skin.','It makes the blanket seem perfectly smooth.','It suggests the blanket smells sweet.','It tells us the blanket is blue.','Scratches is a touch detail; tucking the rough edge away supports discomfort.')
add('sensory detail','Prose: Warm bread filled the kitchen with a yeasty smell. The empty table waited.','Which detail gives the scene a smell rather than a sight?','The yeasty smell of warm bread.','The empty table.','The word kitchen on its own.','The shape of the bread, which is not described.','Yeasty smell directly evokes the nose; the table and room label do not do that.')
add('sensory detail','Poem: Cold rail, trembling hands; / Below, the dark river slides.','Which two senses make this bridge scene vivid?','Touch from the cold rail and sight from the dark river.','Taste from the rail and smell from the river.','Hearing from the hands and taste from the darkness.','Smell from the hands and hearing from the rail.','Cold gives a touch sensation; dark describes the river’s appearance. No taste or smell is supplied.')
# Repetition, rhyme and personification tied to supplied evidence.
add('repetition','Poem: Still we waited by the gate. / Still we watched the empty street. / Still no bus.','What does repeating “Still” emphasise?','The waiting continues without the hoped-for arrival.','Three different buses have arrived.','The children are running down the road.','The street is getting more crowded.','The repeated opening holds the reader in the same unfinished wait.')
add('repetition','Prose: “I can try again,” Sol said after the first miss. After the second, “I can try again.”','How does the repeated speech shape Sol’s character?','It shows determination after setbacks.','It shows Sol refuses to make another attempt.','It proves Sol has already won.','It shows Sol has forgotten the activity.','Repeating the promise after each miss shows continued willingness to try.')
add('rhyme','Poem: A kite climbed high in evening light; / We watched its tail till nearly night.','What effect can the rhyme of “light” and “night” have?','It links the line endings with a matching sound.','It proves both lines have exactly the same number of letters.','It means every word in the poem rhymes.','It changes the kite into the moon.','The shared ending sound gives the two lines an audible connection; rhyme does not require equal letter counts.')
add('personification','Prose: The old gate groaned as Leila pushed it open.','What does “groaned” make the gate seem to do?','Complain like a person while making a creaking noise.','Walk away from Leila.','Tell Leila its age in words.','Open without being touched.','Groaned gives the gate a human-like sound, making its noisy movement more vivid.')
add('repetition','Poem: Home is a light. / Home is a laugh. / Home is the place at the end of our path.','Why repeat “Home is” at the start of each line?','To gather several welcoming images around the idea of home.','To suggest that each line is about a different planet.','To make every ending rhyme with home.','To hide the subject of the poem.','The repeated opening keeps home as the focus while each ending adds a different image.')
add('personification','Poem: The wind tugged my sleeve / And hurried me down the street.','How is the wind presented?','Like someone pulling and urging the speaker along.','Like a person who speaks the speaker’s name.','As a machine with wheels.','As completely still air.','Tugged and hurried give the wind purposeful, person-like actions without claiming it actually speaks.')
add('rhyme','Poem: Beneath a chair, / A teddy bear / Waits for someone’s gentle care.','How do “chair”, “bear” and “care” work together?','Their matching end sounds give the short poem a connected, song-like quality.','They all describe the teddy’s colour.','They are three sound imitations of a real bear.','Their different ending sounds prevent any rhyme.','The three words rhyme, linking the lines through sound rather than copying the same word.')
add('repetition','Prose: First one window glowed. Then another. Then another. Soon the whole street shone.','What does repeating “Then another” help show?','Lights appearing one after another.','All the lights going out at once.','A single window staying dark.','The narrator forgetting where the street is.','The repeated phrase builds a sequence that grows towards the whole street shining.')
# Integrated effects and comparison.
add('combined effects','Poem: Tick, tock. Tick, tock. / Waiting eyes upon the clock.','How do sound imitation and repetition work together?','They make the continuing clock beats noticeable during the wait.','They tell us the exact time without showing any numbers.','They suggest the clock has stopped permanently.','They describe a clock that tastes sweet.','Tick-tock imitates the clock; repeating it makes the ongoing wait feel marked by each beat.')
add('combined effects','Prose: The room went quiet. Then BANG! The door slammed.','Why is the bang especially noticeable here?','It breaks the silence described just before it.','It continues a description of constant shouting.','It tells us the door’s colour.','It makes the door seem to close softly.','The contrast between quiet and a sudden loud sound makes the slam stand out.')
add('combined effects','Poem: Swish, swish, the broom goes by; / Dust lifts like a cloud nearby.','What do the two devices help us imagine?','The broom’s sound and the dust’s cloud-like appearance.','The broom’s taste and the dust’s exact weight.','The broom talking and the dust turning into rain.','The room’s price and the cleaner’s age.','Swish imitates brushing; like a cloud compares the rising dust with a visible shape.')
add('combined effects','Poem: Creep ... creep ... across the stair. / Don’t wake the dog asleep down there.','Which reading suits both the pauses and the meaning?','Slow, soft steps with spaces between them.','Fast shouting with no pauses.','A loud cheer on every word.','Reading only the word dog repeatedly.','The spaced repetition slows the steps, while the sleeping dog explains why quiet matters.')
add('combined effects','Prose A: The waves hit the wall. / Prose B: Boom! Wave after wave hammered the wall.','How does B change the reader’s impression?','It makes the repeated impacts sound forceful.','It makes the water seem perfectly still.','It proves the wall has fallen down.','It tells us how deep the sea is.','Boom, wave after wave and hammered combine sound, repetition and force; the wall’s collapse is not stated.')
add('combined effects','Poem: Softly the silver leaves sway; / Slowly the light slips away.','What do the repeated soft s sounds help support?','A gentle, quiet movement towards evening.','A loud crash in a busy workshop.','A sharp command to run.','A description of a strong taste.','The repeated soft sounds suit softly, slowly and the gradual fading of light; they do not imitate a crash.')
add('combined effects','Prose: The engine coughed twice, then roared awake.','How does the change from “coughed” to “roared” shape the event?','It suggests an uneven start followed by a strong sound.','It means the engine is really a sick lion.','It shows the engine becomes quieter the whole time.','It gives the exact speed of the vehicle.','The first verb suggests interrupted sounds, while roared suggests a stronger continuing start.')
add('combined effects','Poem: Round and round the leaves we chase; / Round and round our windy race.','Which detail best supports a sense of circling movement?','The phrase “Round and round” begins both lines.','The poem names the wind once.','The last words have different spellings.','The lines contain no movement words.','The repeated phrase returns the reader to the same motion, echoing the children circling.')
# Test: independently authored transfer tasks, with no Practice passage reused.
add('rhythm','Poem: Push, glide! Push, glide! / Skates sweep smoothly side to side.','Read the repeated pair with an even beat. What does it echo?','The repeated effort and glide of skating.','A skater standing completely still.','One loud fall followed by silence.','The colour of the skates.','Each return to push and glide echoes another skating movement.')
add('rhythm','Poem: We hurry down the winding lane; / Stop ... / Listen ... / Here comes the train.','What effect do the two short paused lines create?','They interrupt the hurry to make room for careful listening.','They make the train disappear.','They show the children never stop moving.','They tell us the journey lasts two days.','The pauses after stop and listen break the earlier movement, making the change in action felt.')
add('rhythm','Poem: Boom-ba, boom-ba, beat the drum; / Here the marching players come.','Which performance best supports the described marching?','Keep a steady repeated beat through the first line.','Use long, random silences between every sound.','Whisper only the last word and omit the rest.','Read the line backwards.','A regular repeated beat matches marching; the poem does not require random stops.')
add('rhythm','Poem A: Drift ... float ... settle down. / Poem B: Jump! Dash! Race through town!','Which statement compares the effects of the marked pauses and exclamations?','A invites a slower reading; B suggests energetic bursts.','Both poems demand the same hurried rhythm.','A suggests more sudden activity than B.','B is about resting quietly.','The spaced verbs in A encourage slowing, while the short exclamations in B suggest lively action.')
add('onomatopoeia','Prose: Plop! A pebble dropped into the deep bucket. Small circles spread across the water.','What does the sound-word add to this event?','The sound of the pebble entering the water.','The number of circles in the bucket.','The colour of the pebble.','The name of the person holding the bucket.','Plop imitates the small dropping-into-water sound, adding hearing to the visible ripples.')
add('onomatopoeia','Poem: Crunch beneath each boot; / Dry leaves cover every root.','Why does “crunch” suit the setting?','It evokes dry leaves being crushed underfoot.','It suggests the boots are walking through deep water.','It names the tree’s age.','It shows the leaves are silent and untouched.','Crunch imitates the brittle sound of dry leaves breaking beneath boots.')
add('onomatopoeia','Prose: Ding! The lift doors opened. “Our floor,” said Nan.','Which change would remove the direct sound imitation while keeping the event?','The lift made a sound, and its doors opened.','Ding, ding! The lift doors opened.','DING! The lift doors opened wide.','A bright ding rang as the lift opened.','Saying made a sound reports the noise without imitating it; each other choice retains ding.')
add('onomatopoeia','Poem: Rattle, rattle, over stones; / The little cart shakes all my bones.','How does repeating “rattle” affect the journey?','It makes the shaking sound seem to continue over the rough ground.','It shows a single silent step.','It tells us the cart has reached smooth water.','It gives the exact number of stones.','Repeated rattle evokes an ongoing series of noises as the cart travels across stones.')
add('simile','Prose: The red balloon bobbed like a bright cherry above the crowd.','Which feature does the cherry comparison mainly highlight?','The balloon’s round, red appearance.','The balloon’s ability to grow on a tree.','The crowd’s sour taste.','The string’s length.','The shared colour and rounded shape help the reader picture the balloon; it is not literally fruit.')
add('simile','Poem: Her whisper, light as falling snow, / Reached me softly, small and low.','How does the comparison shape the whisper?','It makes it seem delicate and gentle.','It makes it seem loud and harsh.','It proves the speaker is outdoors in winter.','It means no one can hear any words.','Light falling snow supports the soft, delicate quality explicitly described in the next line.')
add('sensory detail','Prose: The icy cup stung his fingers, but the cocoa smelled sweet.','Which pair of senses does the writer use?','Touch and smell.','Sight and hearing.','Taste and hearing.','Sight and taste.','Stinging icy fingers conveys touch; smelled sweet conveys smell. Drinking or tasting is not stated.')
add('sensory detail','Poem: Golden crumbs on a blue plate; / A buttery smell makes breakfast great.','How do the two lines make breakfast vivid?','They combine a visible colour contrast with a smell.','They give only facts about the time.','They describe a loud noise and a rough texture.','They tell us the exact recipe.','Golden and blue create a visual contrast; buttery smell adds a second sensory detail.')
add('repetition','Prose: “Not yet,” whispered the audience as the curtain shook. “Not yet.” Then it rose.','How does the repeated phrase affect the moment before the curtain rises?','It draws attention to waiting for the performance to begin.','It says the performance has already ended.','It describes the colours of the curtain.','It proves nobody wants to watch.','Repeating not yet holds the audience in anticipation before the change announced by then.')
add('personification','Poem: The thirsty garden drank the rain; / Green heads lifted up again.','How is the garden made to seem alive like a person?','It is described as thirsty and drinking.','The poem gives the gardener’s name.','The rain is measured in a cup.','The garden walks into a house.','Thirsty and drank present plants taking up water through person-like needs and actions; walking is not in the poem.')
add('combined effects','Poem: Snip, snip, the scissors sing; / Paper curls like ribbon string.','Which explanation connects each effect with the right words?','Snip evokes cutting sounds; like ribbon helps picture the curls.','Snip describes a sweet smell; ribbon gives the scissors’ weight.','Sing means a person is definitely singing nearby.','Ribbon proves the paper has turned into cloth.','The sound imitation fits scissors cutting, and the comparison describes the paper’s curled shape.')
add('combined effects','Prose: At first the flag hung limp. Then the wind woke it, snapping it high above the oval.','How does the language show a change?','Woke and snapping make the still flag seem suddenly active.','Limp shows the flag is already moving strongly at the start.','The wind is described as a sleeping child in a bed.','The flag stays in exactly the same condition.','The still opening contrasts with person-like waking and a sharp movement, emphasising the wind’s arrival.')
